{"name":"Pine Cone","hex":"#645345","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pine cone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of pinecone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pine_cone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pine Cone (Fabergé egg)","blocks":[{"text":"The Pine Cone egg is a jewelled enameled Easter egg made under the supervision of the Russian jeweller Peter Carl Fabergé in 1900. The Fabergé egg was made for Alexander Kelch, who presented it to his wife, Barbara (Varvara) Kelch-Bazanova."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine_Cone_(Faberg%C3%A9_egg)","wikidata":"Q7195092"},{"title":"Cone (botany)","blocks":[{"text":"A cone, or in formal botanical usage a strobilus, pl.: strobili, is a seed-bearing organ on gymnosperm plants, especially in conifers and cycads. They are usually woody and variously conic, cylindrical, ovoid, to globular, and have scales and bracts arranged around a central axis, but can be fleshy and berry-like. The cones of Pinophyta contain the reproductive structures. The woody cone is the female cone, which produces seeds. The male cone, which produces pollen, is usually ephemeral and much less conspicuous even at full maturity. The name 'cone' derives from Greek konos, which also gave name to the geometric cone. The individual plates of a cone are known as scales. In conifers where the cone develops over more than one year, the first year's growth of a seed scale on the cone, showing up as a protuberance at the end of the two-year-old scale, is called an umbo, while the second year's growth is called the apophysis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Pinus_coulteri_MHNT_Cone.jpg/330px-Pinus_coulteri_MHNT_Cone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cone_(botany)","wikidata":"Q22710"},{"title":"Pine","blocks":[{"text":"Pines ) are a genus of conifers in the family Pinaceae. Pinus is the sole genus in the subfamily Pinoideae. The species are trees or shrubs with their leaves in bunches, usually of 2 to 5 needles. The seeds are carried on woody cones, with two seeds to each cone scale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Pinus_jeffreyi_at_Lassen_Volcanic_National_Park.jpg/330px-Pinus_jeffreyi_at_Lassen_Volcanic_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine","wikidata":"Q12024"},{"title":"Cone","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a cone is a three-dimensional figure that tapers smoothly from a flat base to a point not contained in the base, called the apex or vertex."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Cone_with_labeled_Radius%2C_Height%2C_Angle_and_Side_v2.svg/330px-Cone_with_labeled_Radius%2C_Height%2C_Angle_and_Side_v2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cone","wikidata":"Q42344"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Picea_Pungens_Young_Cones.jpg/120px-Picea_Pungens_Young_Cones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Picea_Pungens_Young_Cones.jpg/250px-Picea_Pungens_Young_Cones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Picea_Pungens_Young_Cones.jpg/120px-Picea_Pungens_Young_Cones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APicea_Pungens_Young_Cones.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Young_Pine_Cone_Center.jpg/250px-Young_Pine_Cone_Center.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/You